{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdfYRkqbHvFhcprUVWNoz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## CLDW Genre Distribution (n=80)\n\n| Genre | Count | % of Corpus |\n|---|---|---|\n| Travelogue | 27 | 33.8% |\n| Guide | 18 | 22.5% |\n| Poetry | 8 | 10.0% |\n| Essay | 7 | 8.8% |\n| Epistle | 6 | 7.5% |\n| Journal | 6 | 7.5% |\n| Survey | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Drama | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Epistle and Journal | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Fiction | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Novel | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Prose Fiction | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Painting | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Miscellany | 1 | 1.3% |\n| **Total** | **80** | **100%** |\n\n**Assessment: uneven distribution.** Travelogue and Guide together account for over 56% of the corpus, reflecting the CLDW's roots in the 18th–19th century tourist/topographical writing tradition around the Lake District. Poetry, Essay, Epistle, and Journal form a \"middle tier\" (6–8 texts each, ~35% combined), while eight genres (Survey, Drama, Fiction, Novel, Prose Fiction, Painting, Miscellany, and the hybrid \"Epistle and Journal\") are each represented by just a single text (~10% combined). So the corpus is heavily skewed toward non-fiction travel/guide writing, with narrative fiction genres (Novel, Fiction, Prose Fiction — only 3 texts combined) notably underrepresented relative to the historical popularity of Lake District-set fiction (e.g., no Ruskin prose fiction beyond his juvenile poem, no Beatrix Potter, etc.)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":57980,"cache_read_input_tokens":9041,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":57980},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"309139aa-dcbe-48a3-8713-b75e17b8bf7b","uuid":"0470e9d1-c56a-4f86-9657-a69f3f213eca","request_id":"req_011CdfYRjqowMyKnXUG